title The captain's wake holds still -- Mind's sign window and the field's landing rhythm
prompt Keaton reported Mystery re-armed and Mind stopped; the helper's read showed Mind refused at the xy-integrated postcondition.
think Mind's lap finished its DirtySet candidate a third time and the supervisor refused at the sign because xy/main advanced during the lap -- and the advancing commits were this seat's own two evening landings. Three derivations of one proven candidate (roughly 930k Codex tokens by the printed counters) have now missed the sign through three different doors: the stale generated page, the rg-less meter, and the moved upstream.
think The refusal itself is right: signing a candidate against a moved upstream without re-checking is how a silent divergence lands. The structural repairs -- the supervisor pulling and re-verifying instead of refusing, or signing before the integration check -- are changes to the user-owned signed supervisor, Keaton's alone. The coordination repair costs nothing: the field lands its turn log before Mind re-arms, then holds every push until Mind's candidate signs.
obs Adopted as this seat's own practice for tonight: after this send, no field push while a Codex lap runs toward its sign, checked by the helper's activity age before any push. A cheaper standing shape -- the supervisor catching up with a pull-and-recheck rather than a refusal -- is named for Keaton as a possible supervisor lap, never taken by this seat.
obs Mystery runs clean at the field's own nib with an empty status -- its re-arm took. Silence is mid-diagnosis of its roster's block buffering, running.
file session-logs/date/20260830/20260830-223624_the-captains-wake-holds-still.kyri this coordination turn's own record
recommend check-in either -- Mind's re-arm paste waits at Keaton's keys, and the field holds its pushes until the sign lands
